From: unjordi <jordi@ciencias.unam.mx>
Subject: [PATCH] hwmon: pwm-fan: optional static PWM kept across suspend

pwm_fan_suspend() unconditionally powers the fan off on suspend-to-RAM:
it zeroes the PWM duty and, via pwm_fan_power_off(force=true), disables
the fan-supply regulator. On a battery-powered handheld that also charges
while deep-suspended (PSCI system suspend, all CPUs powered down), this
leaves the battery cooking with zero airflow during a charge, because no
CPU is up to run any thermal loop and the charger/thermal telemetry lives
in the ADSP firmware, not Linux.

Add an optional module parameter, suspend_pwm. When it is non-zero,
pwm_fan_suspend() keeps the fan running at that static duty (via set_pwm,
which re-enables the fan-supply regulator and applies the duty) instead of
powering it off. The PMIC LPG that drives the PWM keeps emitting the duty
autonomously while the AP is down, so the fan keeps spinning through S2R.
suspend_pwm defaults to 0, which preserves the stock power-off-on-suspend
behaviour for every board.

The knob is deliberately a global module parameter set from userspace via
/sys/module/pwm_fan/parameters/suspend_pwm right before suspend, so the
policy (e.g. "spin only while charging, never on battery, to avoid
draining the pack") stays out of the generic driver. Two conditions must
also hold for the fan to actually keep spinning: the fan-supply regulator
must remain powered in suspend (drop regulator-off-in-suspend on that rail
in DT) and the PWM source must retain its register state across S2R (true
for the PMIC LPG). Neither is provided here; this patch only stops the
driver from tearing the fan down.

This is a static floor, not a temperature-responsive curve: with the AP
down nothing can re-read the battery temperature or re-target the duty.
It is airflow-while-charging, not closed-loop cooling.

Signed-off-by: unjordi <jordi@ciencias.unam.mx>
---
--- a/drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c
+++ b/drivers/hwmon/pwm-fan.c
@@ -24,6 +24,21 @@
 
 #define MAX_PWM 255
 
+/*
+ * Optional static PWM (1..MAX_PWM) to keep the fan spinning across
+ * suspend-to-RAM instead of powering it off. 0 (default) keeps the stock
+ * power-off-on-suspend behaviour. Userspace sets it via
+ * /sys/module/pwm_fan/parameters/suspend_pwm right before suspend (e.g. only
+ * while charging), so a battery-heating deep-sleep charge still gets airflow
+ * where no CPU is up to run the thermal loop. The fan-supply regulator must
+ * stay powered in suspend for this to take effect (DT: drop
+ * regulator-off-in-suspend on that rail).
+ */
+static unsigned int suspend_pwm;
+module_param(suspend_pwm, uint, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(suspend_pwm,
+	"static PWM (1..255) kept across suspend; 0 = power off on suspend (default)");
+
 struct pwm_fan_tach {
 	int irq;
 	atomic_t pulses;
@@ -856,6 +871,14 @@ static int pwm_fan_suspend(struct device *dev)
 			ctx->suspended = false;
 			__set_pwm(ctx, ctx->pwm_awake);
 		}
+	} else if (suspend_pwm) {
+		/*
+		 * Static floor across suspend-to-RAM, set by userspace only while
+		 * charging. Nothing runs the notifier in suspend-to-RAM, so the
+		 * speed is fixed until resume; a charger detach wakes the system
+		 * (battmgr), which re-evaluates it at the next suspend.
+		 */
+		ret = __set_pwm(ctx, min_t(unsigned int, suspend_pwm, MAX_PWM));
 	} else {
 		ret = pwm_fan_power_off(ctx, true);
 	}
